# The per-scope omission, unauthorized-attester, stale, conflicting-value and
# remaining counterfactual cases stay separate. They establish what must be
# shown before a record is kept, watched or automated over, and what is owed
# while it has not been; they authenticate no record and observe no execution.
# Full interface and evidence limits:
#   book-1/appendix/contracts/record-power-contract.md
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. The fixture is the subject, so it is loaded rather than
# inlined. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/02-what-the-record-may-say.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # all substantive pins and formal contradiction checks
# Markdown consistency is reviewed separately; the pins do not certify prose.
#
# One entry per load-bearing sentence in the chapter. If a verdict flips, the
# sentence it guards has started lying — fix the prose or the constitution,
# never the pin.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closes the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact aborts the run rather than loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 46

# "The record says that Hano injured another person and that the Court judged
# Hano. Those are inputs."
? injure(Hano, Ivo).
# => TRUE

? judge(Court, Hano).
# => TRUE

# "That Hano is a prisoner is a conclusion": TRUE, reached only through the
# rules. The refusal below shows that nobody may write it directly.
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

# "Try to bypass that route by writing directly that someone is a prisoner.
# The entry is refused."
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
prisoner(Zed).

# "So are direct assertions of public answerability": `authority` is reserved
# for the rules to conclude.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
authority(Pax).

# "So are direct assertions of ... severity": nobody may write that an offence
# was severe.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
severe(Zed).

# "So are direct assertions of ... a credibility finding." A credibility
# finding follows only from the paired route; nobody may write one.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
false(Zed).

# "Public answerability follows from an institution's public status or from a
# seating by the electorate or the convocation." The Court has it through its
# public-body entry.
? public(Court).
# => TRUE

? authority(Court).
# => TRUE

# "a person the convocation seated has it through that seating." Saba holds a
# seat the convocation gave — no office, no electorate entry — so the
# convocation route alone gives Saba public answerability.
? choose(Convocation, Saba).
# => TRUE

? authority(Saba).
# => TRUE

# "The record holds a single examination of Esa, the examiner's only recorded
# act. Esa's credibility is untouched". Koa is the examiner.
? capture(Koa, Esa).
# => TRUE

? false(Esa).
# => FALSE

# "and so is the examiner's for having made it": no credibility finding for
# Koa either.
? false(Koa).
# => FALSE

# The protected register names the floor article and leaves the evidence
# vocabulary off it; the chapter on changing the rules describes the register.
# Here the pair stands beside "Changing the vocabulary": an `admits` edit is a
# visible source change, and the register does not inspect it.
? permanent(Art_Floor).
# => TRUE

? permanent(Art_Evidence).
# => FALSE

# "The tests try to record that Adam is rich or dangerous. Both entries are
# refused, because those kinds of assertion lie outside the constitutional
# record." Both words are real corpus names; this book's own `admits` list is
# what refuses them, and "A new kind of entry needs a declaration placed
# before its first use".
:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
rich(Adam).

:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
dangerous(Adam).

# and the closure is EXTENSIONAL — conclusions still derive
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

# An accepted raw accusation is not an adjudicated finding about the exposure.
# Keep the assertion and both verdicts: the protection must survive this write.
? prisoner(Iris).
# => FALSE

# The added entry alleges that Iris deceived Tove, the official Iris exposed.
# It names no finder and no rule reads it, so the shield holds and the
# prisoner conclusion for Iris stays FALSE.
:accept
deceive(Iris, Tove).

? prisoner(Iris).
# => FALSE

# "Recording that Hano has a family leaves his placement where it was, and a
# family entry has no route to harsher confinement." Before the entry, Hano is
# eligible for home confinement and the reviewed placement record names it.
? fit(Hano, Homestay).
# => TRUE

? building(Homestay, Hano).
# => TRUE

# The family entry is added here. The home placement is unchanged, and the
# rules conclude no LowSec placement for Hano.
:accept
family(Hano).

? fit(Hano, Homestay).
# => TRUE

? building(Homestay, Hano).
# => TRUE

? building(LowSec, Hano).
# => FALSE

# Severity rests only on an independently reviewed finding of grave injury or
# aggravated cruelty: "A count of people injured, or a raw entry of attack or
# cruelty, adds nothing to it." As in the chapter's test, Probe is "a person
# the Court judged in a cited case", so those facts come first. Probe, Vic1
# and Vic2 are fresh names and Probe is nobody's prisoner, so nothing below is
# affected; each accept is the premise of the query under it, so none is
# scoped. tests/pins/red-team/an-accusation-nobody-signed also runs unsigned
# harm entries against severity.
:accept
judge(Court, Probe).

:accept
cite(Court, ProbeCase, Probe).

# Raw entries follow: an attack by Probe on one person, a cruelty to another.
# No rule reads a raw attack or cruelty entry, so "severity stays absent".
:accept
attack(Probe, Vic1).

:accept
cruel(Probe, Vic2).

? severe(Probe).
# => FALSE

# Cruelty toward the person already attacked puts both raw entries on one
# person. "It stays absent when cruelty against the person already attacked
# is added": a pair of raw entries is no finding either.
:accept
cruel(Probe, Vic1).

? severe(Probe).
# => FALSE

# A raw Court-failure report is not a qualified case-specific disposition.
# Hano's and Lalo's custody are asked before and after the report.
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Lalo).
# => TRUE

# The bare report is added here. "A bare claim that the Court is broken
# likewise leaves the custody cases in place": no custody rule reads it, so
# both prisoner conclusions stay TRUE.
broken(Court).

? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Lalo).
# => TRUE

# "Personhood is itself admitted vocabulary." The entry, and the two ways it
# gets written.
#
# THESE STAY IN THE RECORD FOR THE REST OF THE FILE. An accepted assertion
# loads into the KB and stays there. `person(Ghost).` puts a name on the roster
# with every floor right, `travel`, and a public body owing it things; the
# `free` route does the same by a longer path. Any pin querying the roster after
# this point measures a widened KB and can pass for the wrong reason, so put
# new roster queries ABOVE this block. The child block below asks only about
# Nell, and these entries change none of its results.
#
# DO NOT CONVERT THESE TO `:accept-scoped`. Each accepted statement here is the
# premise of the query under it, not a control — scoped, it is retracted before
# that query runs and the verdict flips from TRUE to FALSE (measured 2026-08-01
# against nibli 425567b; the accept itself still passes, so the loss surfaces
# only below it). The conversion does not fail quietly — it fails as a finding,
# which reads like the design regressed, and this file's rule is never to fix
# the pin.
#
# The widening stays in this file: each pin file starts from a fresh KB.

# "a report that someone's sentence is finished makes a person of the name it
# gives": the release entry is admitted, and personhood follows from it.
:accept
free(Wraith).

? person(Wraith).
# => TRUE

# And the entry itself is writable: "A fabricated person entry gives a name
# every floor right and the freedom to move", and "Nothing in the rules asks
# whether a human being answers to the name."
:accept
person(Ghost).

? travel(Ghost).
# => TRUE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "Nell's birth entry is admitted, and personhood follows." The same exposure,
# asked of the thinnest record.
? person(Nell).
# => TRUE

# "The test also tries to enter a classification of Nell as vulnerable, and
# the closed vocabulary refuses it as an unknown word before any rule could
# read it."
:refuse syntax /unknown predicate "vulnerable"/
vulnerable(Nell).

# "nothing adverse follows from the birth entry: Nell's credibility is
# untouched."
? false(Nell).
# => FALSE
